\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Preferences and Choice When Alternatives Are Uncertain Prospects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
August 29, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The experiment I am proposing is concerned with preferences, utility, and
 choice when alternatives are uncertain prospects.
 Essentially, it will focus on the comparisons of lotteries, or distributions,
 in terms of risk and return.
 We wish to see when is a lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 unambiguously better than a lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 for a money monotone expectued utility maximizer.
 According to the theory, there are two natural ways that random outcomes
 can be compared: first-order stochastic dominance (FSD) and second-order
 stochastic dominance (SSD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First off, let us consider two lotteries 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with compact support.
 By definition, lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 first-order stochastically dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq_{FSD}$
\end_inset

, if for each money monotone expected utility maximizer, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, if for each non-decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int u\ dF\geq\int u\ dG
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, the focus of FSD is the level of returns for each lottery.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we once again consider two lotteries 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with compact support.
 Lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 second-order stochastically dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, denoted, 
\begin_inset Formula $\geq_{SSD}$
\end_inset

, if for each money monotone risk averse expected utility maximizer, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, if for each concave non-decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int u\ dF\geq\int u\ dG
\]

\end_inset

Now, when 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{F}=\mu_{G}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F\geq_{SSD}G$
\end_inset

 if and only if for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\int_{-\infty}^{x}F(t)\ dt\leq\int_{-\infty}^{x}G(t)\ dt
\]

\end_inset

Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $F\geq_{SSD}G$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is a mean preserving spread of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 So, the focus of second-order stochastic dominance is the dispersion of
 returns.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As one can see, the trade-off between returns and risk has been omitted
 from the model.
 Thus, the goal of the experiment is to discover empirical regularities
 when lab participants are comparing lotteries with both different means
 and variances.
 The primary question I wish to answer is: Under what conditions will lab
 participants prefer a lottery with a slightly lower mean and variance to
 one with a higher mean and variance? Since most individuals are naturally
 risk averse, then I will focus on the issues addressed by SSD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To test this hypothesis, I will first verify that the prevailing theory
 holds in the labratory setting.
 So, participants will be presented with two lotteries that have the same
 mean but differing variances.
 The theory predicts that if both participants are risk averse expected
 utility maximizers, then they will choose the less risky lottery, or the
 one with the smaller variance.
 Next, I will raise the mean of one lottery while proportionately lowering
 the variance of the other, and I will record which lottery is chosen.
 I want to see whether there is a trend in which one lottery is favored
 over another.
 If so, are we able analyze the propensity for this happening? In addition,
 using the listed mean and variances of the two lotteries, can we predict
 which one is chosen? Finally, what are the limits in which the lottery
 with the higher mean and variance is prefered, i.e., past a certain threshold,
 the lottery with the lower mean and variance is then chosen?
\end_layout

